> Verify that binary serialization formats are not used unless they enforce schema validation and disallow arbitrary type information to prevent deserialization vulnerabilities.

I believe there are several things which do not work here.

We should focus on the goal which is "verify that deserialization cannot be used by attacker to trigger RCE".

There are several ways this can be achieved:

* serialization is only used internally (this case is important as discussed below!);
* serialization is used only for MAC-ed / signed message (→ I don't really trust this approach because obtaining the secret-or-private key would grant [arbitrary code execution power](https://github.com/ch4n3-yoon/django-pickleserializer-rce-poc));
* deserialization cannot trigger arbitrary code execution (which is what is required here).

In Java, many mechanisms are based on Java serialization with arbitrary type information and I don't believe this requirement is realistic in this case. Some examples:

* Java servlet sessions: this is use case is safe because the serialized data is only used internally in the same tier of the application;
* [Spark](https://spark.apache.org/docs/3.5.2/tuning.html#data-serialization), in this case the serialized data is only used within the nodes of the Spark cluster.

